---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";

import tablet from "../assets/tablet-01-main-screen.png";
import editor from "../assets/tablet-03-main-screen-editor.png";
import search from "../assets/tablet-06-search-symbol.png";
import phone from "../assets/phone-01-main-screen.png";
import addWord from "../assets/phone-04-add-word.png";
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>GibTalk</h1>
      <p class="tagline">{SITE.tagline}</p>
      <p>
        Tap a picture, and GibTalk says the word out loud. For children and
        adults who can't yet speak, or can't speak reliably, it's a voice, and a
        way to be understood. Free, with no ads, on Android, iPhone and iPad.
      </p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="button" href={SITE.playStore}>
          Get it on Google Play
        </a>
        <a class="button green" href={SITE.appStore}>
          Download on the App Store
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Image
    src={tablet}
    alt="GibTalk on a tablet: a grid of picture tiles such as Greetings, Food, Exercise, Yes, No, Stop and Good Job"
    widths={[640, 1100]}
    sizes="(max-width: 58rem) 100vw, 58rem"
    class="shot wide"
    loading="eager"
  />

  <h2>What is AAC?</h2>
  <p>
    AAC stands for <em>augmentative and alternative communication</em>. An AAC
    app is a grid of pictures on a tablet or phone. Tap <em>drink</em>, and it
    says "drink". Tap <em>I want</em>, then <em>play</em>, and it says "I want
    play". Speech therapists and teachers often recommend one for children who
    are autistic, or who have other conditions that affect speech.
  </p>

  <h2>What GibTalk does</h2>
  <ul class="features">
    <li>
      <strong>Pictures that speak.</strong> Every word is a picture tile, spoken
      aloud with your device's own voice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Any language, per word.</strong> Mix Malay and English, or any
      languages your device can speak, on the same screen. Each word is spoken
      in its own language.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own words.</strong> Add and edit words, organise them into
      folders, and copy and paste between folders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own pictures.</strong> Search thousands of free symbols, or
      take a photo of the real thing: your child's own cup, bag or car.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ready-made word sets.</strong> Start from
      <a href="/templates/">templates</a> for greetings, mealtime, play, the
      playground and more, in English and Malay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A keyboard that talks,</strong> for typing a sentence and hearing
      it spoken.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backup and restore,</strong> to keep your words safe or move them
      to a new device.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="shots">
    <Image
      src={editor}
      alt="Editing mode, with Backup, Restore, Templates and Add buttons"
      widths={[480, 800]}
      sizes="(max-width: 40rem) 90vw, 26rem"
      class="shot"
    />
    <Image
      src={search}
      alt="Searching for symbols to use as a word's picture"
      widths={[480, 800]}
      sizes="(max-width: 40rem) 90vw, 26rem"
      class="shot"
    />
    <Image
      src={phone}
      alt="GibTalk on a phone"
      widths={[240, 480]}
      sizes="12rem"
      class="shot narrow"
    />
    <Image
      src={addWord}
      alt="Adding a word on a phone"
      widths={[240, 480]}
      sizes="12rem"
      class="shot narrow"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>Private by design</h2>
  <p>
    GibTalk works offline. There's no account, no login, no analytics and no
    data collected. Your words and pictures stay on your device. Read the
    <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Free, and staying free</h2>
  <p>
    Most good AAC apps cost a lot, and many families wait for a sale before they
    can buy one. GibTalk is free, with no ads and no paid features. Its only
    running cost is a small server for searching symbols. The code is
    <a href={SITE.github}>open source</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Made by a parent</h2>
  <p>
    I built GibTalk for my own kids, and shaped it with their teachers and
    speech therapist. <a href={SITE.story}>Read how it started</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Help</h2>
  <h3>A word is spoken in the wrong language</h3>
  <p>
    Edit the word and choose its language. If it still sounds wrong, your device
    may need that language's text-to-speech voice installed, usually in your
    device's settings under text-to-speech or accessibility.
  </p>
  <h3>Moving to a new device</h3>
  <p>
    In edit mode, tap <strong>Backup</strong> to save your words to a file, then
    <strong>Restore</strong> it on the new device. Restore replaces the words
    already on that device.
  </p>
  <p>
    Something else? <a href={SITE.issues}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 2rem;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .features {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .features li + li {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }
  :global(.shot) {
    border: 3px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 16px;
  }
  :global(.shot.wide) {
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .shots {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .shots :global(.shot) {
    width: min(26rem, 100%);
  }
  .shots :global(.shot.narrow) {
    width: 12rem;
  }
</style>
